        <h2 id="why">Why people look for a Udemy alternative in {currentYear}</h2>
        <p>
          The complaints have not changed much in five years. What has changed is the evidence behind them — and the ownership.
        </p>

        <h3>Quality is a lottery</h3>
        <p>
          There is no editorial gate on Udemy. Class Central's January 2026 analysis of the full catalog found 272,000+ courses, a median of just 243 enrollments per course, and a top 20% of courses that collects 91% of all enrollments. Ratings help, but a 4.6 on a course last updated in 2019 is a warning, not a recommendation.
        </p>

        <h3>The prices are fiction until the sale starts</h3>
        <p>
          Around 39% of Udemy courses carry a list price above $200, yet the realistic price is $10 to $20 during the near-constant promotions. That is fine once you know the game, but it trains you to distrust every number on the site. The honest move is to decide what a course is worth to you and never pay list price.
        </p>

        <h3>The certificate is decorative</h3>
        <p>
          A Udemy certificate of completion is a PDF with your name on it. It is not accredited, not proctored, and not recognized by employers the way a Google or IBM Professional Certificate is. If a credential is the reason you are learning, Udemy is the wrong tool.
        </p>

        <h3>Courses are getting shorter, not deeper</h3>
        <p>
          Average course length has fallen to about 3.8 hours, down from 7.7 hours in early 2023. Some of that is healthy — fewer 40-hour marathons — but a lot of it is two-hour "complete" courses that stop where the hard part begins. Structured platforms such as Codecademy, DataCamp and Zero to Mastery force a curriculum on you, which is what most self-taught learners actually need.
        </p>

        <h3>The Personal Plan is not the whole catalog</h3>
        <p>
          Udemy's subscription starts around $13/month and covers a curated set of 28,000+ courses, not the full marketplace. A Personal Plan bought on udemy.com also does not get the 30-day refund that individual courses do. If you were hoping for a Netflix of Udemy, read our <a class="inline-link" href="/udemy-personal-plan">Personal Plan review</a> before you pay.
        </p>

