          <li><strong>Pricing you can predict.</strong> Subscriptions with a posted annual price beat marketplaces with rotating sales. Check whether the headline price is billed annually, whether it renews at a higher rate (Domestika and Educative both do), and whether a free tier exists.</li>
          <li><strong>Credential value.</strong> Only a handful of online credentials move a resume: Coursera's Google, IBM and Meta Professional Certificates; edX MicroMasters and university certificates; vendor certifications that Pluralsight prepares you for; and Udacity Nanodegrees, which are respected in tech hiring because they are project-graded. Everything else, Udemy included, is proof of effort rather than proof of skill.</li>
          <li><strong>Hands-on practice.</strong> Watching someone code is not learning to code. Codecademy, DataCamp, Educative and Pluralsight run exercises and labs in the browser; Udacity and Zero to Mastery grade or review real projects. Udemy is video with the occasional quiz.</li>
          <li><strong>Currency of content.</strong> Ask how the platform handles a course that goes stale. Subscription platforms retire and rebuild courses; on a marketplace, a 2017 course keeps selling as long as its rating holds. For AI tooling, cloud services or JavaScript frameworks, this matters more than any other criterion.</li>
